\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Certified congruent numbers up to a seed bound.} Enumerate admissible seeds with
$m\le M$, compute $\Ar(m,n)$ and its squarefree part, and collect the results. Every output
is a congruent number, and every congruent number with a witness of area at most $M^2$
appears. Cost: $O(M^2)$ seeds.

\paragraph{Tree walk and address.} The three moves are invertible on admissible seeds: the
parent of $(m,n)$ is determined by comparing $m$ with $2n$ and $n$ with $2(m-n)$. Iterating
the inverse until reaching $(2,1)$ returns the unique word in $\{A,B,C\}$ addressing a node,
and hence the depth at which a congruent number first becomes visible. Cost: $O(\log m)$
steps.

\paragraph{Triangle $\leftrightarrow$ curve point.} Implement the two explicit maps with
exact rational arithmetic; composed with the group law on $E_s$ they generate new rational
triangles of area $s$ from old ones --- the tree-side meaning of a point of infinite order.

\section{Discussion}

The classical phrasing quantifies over all rational triangles; the main theorem replaces it
by the statement that the congruent numbers are the squarefree parts of the values of
$mn(m-n)(m+n)$ on a set enumerated bijectively by a ternary tree. Three consequences are
structural. First, there is no redundancy: by Barning--Hall uniqueness, each congruent number
corresponds to a well-defined set of addresses, so the multiplicity of a square class is a
combinatorial invariant. Second, descent is intrinsic: the descent parameter is the first
Euclid coordinate, and a single template (four coprime factors, a case analysis modulo $8$,
the substitution $u=\tfrac{d+c}2$, $v=\tfrac{d-c}2$) yields all three unconditional laws.
Third, statistics become finitary: properness turns questions such as ``how many congruent
numbers below $X$ does the tree certify at depth $\le d$?'' into finite computations, and the
increment identities bound the required depth.

The equivalence is faithful, so the tree cannot be easier than the problem: deciding whether
a squarefree $s$ is congruent remains equivalent to deciding whether $E_s$ has positive rank.
What changes is the kind of question one can pose --- not ``does a point exist?'' but ``how
often does the quartic $mn(m-n)(m+n)$ revisit a square class along a walk in the tree?''.
Even a negative answer, namely perfect equidistribution of square classes, would be a sharp
theorem quantifying why the triangle side alone cannot see the rank.

Finally, the unconditional results here are \emph{descent-provable}: they use only coprime
factorisation, quadratic residues modulo $8$, and well-foundedness of the tree. Since the
case analysis is purely local --- which of the four factors receives a prime, and the prime's
residue modulo $8$ --- it is natural to expect a finite decision table, indexed by the mod-$8$
residues of the prime divisors of a squarefree $s$, that captures the entire descent-provable
portion of the congruent number problem.

\section{Future work}

\begin{enumerate}
\item Generalise the coprime splitting lemma from a prime to a squarefree modulus with a
prescribed distribution of its prime factors among $m$, $n$, $m-n$, $m+n$, thereby
mechanising the case analysis and producing the full mod-$8$ table; expected consequences
include the non-congruence of $2p$ for $p\equiv3\pmod 8$ and of suitable products $pq$.
\item Construct a tree-side $2$-descent invariant $\kappa$ with values in
$(\Z/8)^\times\times(\Z/8)^\times$, computable from the seed modulo $16$, on which the three
generators act through a fixed permutation representation.
\item Study the distribution of squarefree parts of $2t^2\pm t$ along the Pell spine.
\item Define $D(s)$ as the least depth of a node whose area has squarefree part $s$, compute
it for small congruent numbers, and compare with canonical heights of generators of
$E_s(\Q)$.
\item Estimate the number of squarefree $s\le X$ certified at depth at most $d$, aiming at a
first tree-side density theorem.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

The area function $mn(m-n)(m+n)$ on the Berggren tree of primitive Pythagorean triples is not
an accessory of the tree: it is the congruent number problem. Its values, read modulo squares,
are exactly the congruent numbers, and the same data is exactly the set of rational points
with $x>0$, $y\ne 0$ on the curves $y^2=x^3-s^2x$. The tree carries its own descent, strong
enough to prove unconditionally that $1$ and $2$ are not congruent and that no prime
$p\equiv3\pmod 8$ is congruent. The area function increases strictly along each branch,
multiplies by more than six along the middle branch, obeys the silver law along the Pell
spine, and is proper. Explicit, enumerable, descent-friendly and statistically well-posed,
the tree is a laboratory in which one of the oldest open problems in number theory can be
experimented on with exact arithmetic.

\end{document}

